Bundled supplementary dictionary:
  dic-nico-intersection-pixiv  (mozc/src/main/assets/dictionaries/)
  https://github.com/ncaq/dic-nico-intersection-pixiv
  The project's code is MIT licensed. Its author states that no copyright is
  claimed over the generated dictionary itself, as it is derived from scraped
  article titles of Niconico Pedia and Pixiv Encyclopedia. That is the upstream
  author's own statement rather than a formal licence grant; redistributors
  should satisfy themselves it fits their situation.

  google-ime-user-dictionary-ja-en  (mozc/src/main/assets/dictionaries/)
  https://github.com/KEINOS/google-ime-user-dictionary-ja-en
  Katakana loanword to English spelling, derived from the EDICT project.
  The dictionary data is under Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0, the
  same terms as EDICT itself.

  mozcdic-ut-jawiki  (mozc/src/main/assets/dictionaries/)
  https://github.com/utuhiro78/mozcdic-ut-jawiki
  Readings and surface forms derived from the Japanese Wikipedia.
  Licensed under Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike, the terms Wikipedia
  content carries. The file shipped here is reformatted from the upstream
  distribution — the part-of-speech, connection-id and cost columns are dropped,
  since upstream leaves them as placeholders — and that reformatting is itself
  distributed under the same terms. No entry text is altered.

  Those terms cover the dictionary, not software that uses it: the upstream
  project states explicitly that an application bundling this dictionary need
  not adopt a Creative Commons licence, and asks only that it say it uses this
  community-built open-source dictionary. This notice is that statement.
  zinna-IME itself remains Apache 2.0.
